From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 23:23:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Sink rejection: prompt logprobs go out with the request's
 first output

The step that finishes a prompt samples the request's first token, and
with sink rejection (on by default for DeepSeek V4.1) that token is held
back as the next step's draft zero. The step therefore has no output for
the request: new_token_ids is empty, the request has not stopped, and
update_from_output emits nothing. The V2 runner's PromptLogprobsWorker
returns the prompt's logprobs in that same step, so the scheduler's
"no partial prefill outputs" assertion failed and the engine core died
(EngineDeadError, HTTP 500) on any request with prompt_logprobs and more
than one token to generate. A request whose first token ends it (one
token, a stop token) commits that token at once and was not affected.

The scheduler now keeps such a step's prompt logprobs on the request and
emits them with the request's first output, beside the token, as they go
out without sink rejection. A request preempted while its first token is
still held has committed nothing, so its resumed prompt returns its
logprobs again; the new ones replace those held. The assertion still
holds for every other step without output.

Tests: the scripted V2-runner worker of the sink tests returns prompt
logprobs as PromptLogprobsWorker does (accumulated over chunks, skipped
for a request resumed after its prompt). The prompt logprobs go out once,
in the request's first output, with sync and async scheduling, with and
without drafts and chunked prompts; the request's tokens and its
neighbour's are those of the same run without prompt logprobs; through
the output processor one response carries a logprob for every prompt
token after the first; across a preemption with the token held the
resumed prompt's logprobs replace the held ones. All fail on the
assertion without the change.
